#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""keydrop-home-test — the always-on page holds the vault's line without a TTL.

Never touches a real tailnet or a real keychain: `tailscale` is a stub that
keeps its serve config in a JSON file and logs every argv it's given, and
`security` is the fake in tests/fakes.py, backed by another JSON file.
Requests go straight to the page's 127.0.0.1 port carrying the headers serve
would set, which is also how a spoof would arrive. Config comes from the
environment only (KEYDROP_CONFIG points at a file that doesn't exist).

What's asserted:

1. No stored value ever appears in a response body, keydrop.log,
   home-audit.log, or anywhere in the state dir; the path token never lands in
   the state dir, a log, or the stub's argv log.
2. The login pin: missing, wrong, doubled, underscore-beside and
   underscore-only Tailscale-User-Login all get a bare 404 and store nothing.
3. CSRF: missing, forged, other-site Origin and duplicated Origin are refused
   and count; a real-but-aged form token asks for a reload and doesn't count;
   a cross-site Sec-Fetch-Site never reaches the handler and doesn't count; a
   GET carrying the add fields changes nothing.
4. Lockout: MAX_BAD strikes end the process, take the route down, and the
   lock survives a restart (the respawned page refuses to serve) until
   --unlock.
5. The write rate limit holds, and holds across a restart.
6. --stop tears the route down (with the process alive, and after a SIGKILL
   left the route behind), and the page stays down until --unlock; the page's
   own Stop button does the same.
7. Delete is scoped to <service prefix><NAME_RE> under the configured account.
8. --new-link replaces the old link without a restart.
9. Review fixes. A request without the owner's login (wrong, missing,
   underscore, doubled; any path, any method, GET or POST) is a bare 404 and
   never a strike, while the owner's login with a wrong path token or method
   still counts.
   A form left open across a restart is stale, not a strike; forged and
   garbled tokens still count. A stop flag ends a running page by itself,
   and a --stop landing mid start-up (the stub stalls `status --json` to hold
   that window open) leaves no page and no route. The rendered plist
   template lints and runs the installed copy with `home`.
10. Round two. A strike needs the owner's login and Sec-Fetch-Site same-origin or
   none; without Fetch Metadata a failure is a bare 404. `home --http` is
   refused and the no-certs error doesn't offer it. A peer without the
   owner's login doesn't spend the owner's per-minute budget, and its refusals are logged
   at most REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE a minute. The documented install line, run
   in a throwaway HOME, installs origin/main and not the working tree.
11. Round three. Every page answers only an exact Host allowlist: a
   rebinding Host is a bare 404, uncharged and unstruck, on home and on a
   one-shot request.
12. Same-port binding. tailscaled's netstack forwards tailnet TCP to
   127.0.0.1:<port> for any port serve isn't claiming, so the loopback port
   always equals the tailnet port and nothing listens before the route
   exists: the stub probes the backend when `serve` adds a route and records
   whether it was already listening (it must not be). claim_port refuses a
   port serve already uses rather than drift. home listens on
   127.0.0.1:HOME_PORT and nothing else, refuses --local, and its watchdog
   closes the listener and exits 1 when the route disappears or is
   repointed, without firing while the route is intact. HOME_PORT is a free
   port the suite picks, never the default 9443.
13. Round five. Every request, on home, request and vault pages alike,
   checks the route before the Host allowlist and the login pin. With the
   route dropped (or repointed, or `serve status` garbled) the page stays up
   past the drop, so the idle watchdog isn't what ends it, and then a request
   carrying a valid Host, the owner's login and the right token gets a bare 404, no
   strike, and the page exits (home with 1). Unreadable or garbled status
   counts as gone. A repointed route is left in place by teardown.

    tests/keydrop-home-test
"""
import html, importlib.machinery, importlib.util, json, os, re, secrets, shlex, signal, subprocess, sys
import tempfile, time, urllib.error, urllib.parse, urllib.request

